<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US">
  <docinfo><directories external="../assets"/></docinfo>
  <book xml:id="finite-maps">
    <title>Finite Morphisms: Two Elementary Examples</title>
    <shorttitle>Finite morphisms</shorttitle>
    <frontmatter xml:id="finite-metadata">
      <bibinfo><author><personname>Jie Fu</personname></author></bibinfo>
      <titlepage><titlepage-items/></titlepage>
    </frontmatter>
    <chapter xml:id="finite-power-maps">
      <title>A power map</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>We work over <m>\mathbb C</m>. Two explicit ring inclusions will exhibit a finite morphism and the normalization of a singular curve.</p>
      </introduction>
      <section xml:id="finite-definition">
        <title>Finiteness on affine schemes</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>A ring map <m>R\to D</m> is <em>finite</em> if <m>D</m> is finitely generated as an <m>R</m>-module.<fn xml:id="finite-module-footnote">A finitely generated module may have infinitely many elements.</fn> A scheme morphism is finite if the inverse image of every affine open is affine and the corresponding coordinate algebra is a finite module. For <m>\operatorname{Spec}D\to\operatorname{Spec}R</m>, checking the module condition on <m>R\to D</m> suffices; see <xref ref="stacks-finite"/>, Definition 29.45.1 and Lemma 29.45.3.</p>
        </introduction>
        <subsection xml:id="power-map">
          <title>A basis over the target ring</title>
          <p xml:id="anchor-power-basis">Fix an integer <m>n\geq1</m>. The homomorphism <m>\mathbb C[s]\to\mathbb C[t]</m> sending <m>s</m> to <m>t^n</m> defines the power map on the affine line.</p>
          <aside xml:id="margin-power-basis">
            <p>Every nonnegative integer has a unique expression <m>qn+r</m> with <m>q\geq0</m> and <m>0\leq r\leq n-1</m>. The remainder selects a basis vector.</p>
          </aside>
          <proposition xml:id="prop-power-free">
            <statement>
              <p>Under this homomorphism, <m>\mathbb C[t]</m> is a free <m>\mathbb C[s]</m>-module of rank <m>n</m>, with basis <m>1,t,\ldots,t^{n-1}</m>. Consequently, the power map is finite.</p>
            </statement>
            <proof xml:id="proof-power-free">
              <p>Write <m>g(t)=\sum_{k\geq0}c_kt^k</m>, with only finitely many nonzero coefficients. For each remainder <m>r</m>, put <m>h_r(s)=\sum_{q\geq0}c_{qn+r}s^q</m>. Grouping exponents modulo <m>n</m> gives</p>
              <md number="no">g(t)=\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}t^rh_r(t^n).</md>
              <p>This proves generation. For independence, expand a relation <m>\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}t^rh_r(t^n)=0</m>. Terms from distinct pairs <m>(q,r)</m> have distinct exponents <m>qn+r</m>, so every coefficient vanishes. Thus every <m>h_r</m> is zero. This also covers <m>n=1</m>, when the basis is <m>1</m>.</p>
            </proof>
          </proposition>
        </subsection>
      </section>
    </chapter>
    <chapter xml:id="finite-cusp">
      <title>The cusp</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>The next finite generation argument uses the same monomial bookkeeping as <xref ref="prop-power-free"/>. Set <m>A=\mathbb C[t^2,t^3]\subset B=\mathbb C[t]</m>.</p>
      </introduction>
      <section xml:id="cusp-ring">
        <title>The coordinate ring and its normalization</title>
        <subsection xml:id="cusp-equation">
          <title>The equation of the cusp</title>
          <proposition xml:id="prop-cusp-equation">
            <statement>
              <p>The substitutions <m>x\mapsto t^2</m> and <m>y\mapsto t^3</m> induce an isomorphism</p>
              <md number="no">\mathbb C[x,y]/(y^2-x^3)\cong A.</md>
            </statement>
            <proof xml:id="proof-cusp-equation">
              <p>The relation maps to zero, and the map is surjective by the definition of <m>A</m>. Division by the monic polynomial <m>y^2-x^3</m>, viewed as a polynomial in <m>y</m>, gives every quotient class a unique representative <m>a(x)+yb(x)</m>. Its image is <m>a(t^2)+t^3b(t^2)</m>. The first summand has only even exponents and the second only odd exponents. If their sum is zero, both polynomials vanish, forcing <m>a=b=0</m>. Hence the induced map is injective.</p>
            </proof>
          </proposition>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="cusp-normalization">
          <title>A finite birational map</title>
          <p xml:id="anchor-cusp-normalization">The integral closure of a domain <m>A</m> in <m>\operatorname{Frac}A</m> consists of elements satisfying monic equations over <m>A</m>. Its spectrum is the normalization of <m>\operatorname{Spec}A</m>.</p>
          <aside xml:id="margin-cusp-normalization">
            <p>A birational map of integral affine schemes induces an isomorphism of their fraction fields. Here the missing coordinate is recovered as <m>t=t^3/t^2</m>.</p>
          </aside>
          <proposition xml:id="prop-cusp-normalization">
            <statement>
              <p>The inclusion <m>A\subset B</m> defines a finite birational morphism <m>\operatorname{Spec}B\to\operatorname{Spec}A</m>. It is the normalization morphism and is not an isomorphism.</p>
            </statement>
            <proof xml:id="proof-cusp-normalization">
              <p>Every exponent <m>k\geq2</m> is even or is <m>3</m> plus an even number. Thus <m>t^k\in A</m>, and every polynomial belongs to <m>A+At</m>. This proves finiteness. The fraction fields agree because <m>t=t^3/t^2\in\operatorname{Frac}A</m>.</p>
              <p>The ring <m>B</m> is a principal ideal domain and is integrally closed: if a reduced fraction <m>f/g</m> satisfies a monic equation of degree <m>d</m> over <m>B</m>, clearing denominators shows that <m>g</m> divides <m>f^d</m>. Coprimality forces <m>g</m> to be a unit.</p>
              <p>A finite ring extension is integral; see <xref ref="stacks-finite"/>, Lemma 29.45.4. Hence <m>B</m> lies in the integral closure of <m>A</m>. Conversely, an element of their common fraction field integral over <m>A</m> is integral over <m>B</m>, since the same monic equation has coefficients in <m>B</m>. It therefore belongs to <m>B</m>, proving the normalization claim.</p>
              <p>Every polynomial in <m>t^2,t^3</m> has zero coefficient of <m>t</m>, so <m>t\notin A</m>. The ring inclusion is not surjective, and its induced morphism of affine schemes is not an isomorphism.</p>
            </proof>
          </proposition>
        </subsection>
      </section>
    </chapter>
    <backmatter>
